WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:04.470 align:start position:0%
 
something's gone  very wrong in the fabric of american  society which  leaves people vulnerable to the appeal  of  the political hate industry hi my name  is david smick and i'm the writer  director  of the film stars and strife the academy  has always been at the forefront  of political and social issues and i  don't think there is a  more timely film given the recent  presidential election in fact the  election confirms  everything said in the film that america  is divided pretty evenly divided  but that the public seems to not trust  either party for very long  americans are also saying that we want  our leaders  to stop the hate show in washington we  want them to start solving problems  we're tired of the dysfunction we want  our leaders to start to put  country before party that's the powerful  message of the movie and that was the  message of the election  a time to build a time to reap and a  time to sow  and a time to heal this is the time to  heal in america  so i wanted to reach people with this  kind of common sense message of  let's stop the hate it doesn't mean we  have to agree on everything but this was  a nation that was  founded on compromise and compromise is  essential  for us to survive so i went to  some of the top documentary filmmakers  in the country  and i said i want to do a documentary  on hate and dysfunction and the collapse  of empathy  in american life and politics i want to  know how we got here  and how we get out of this mess and  everybody turn me down  and then i went to a fellow baltimorean  barry levinson who also grew up in a  working-class section of baltimore and  then i said  can you recommend someone who could do  this film  and his response was look you should do  the film you've  you have written two new york times  bestsellers you obviously know how to  tell a story  he right from the beginning said this  should be a full-length film  because this this subject is just too  too important i went at it saying  okay i'm going to deal with the  complexity of the answer and of the  dilemma  so how do you do that in a documentary  film  without putting people to sleep  we did a cut of the film i went back to  to barry levinson and barry looked at it  and said this is very compelling  and he made some editorial suggestions  and we had a screening by the way  on the first cut of the film in in  hollywood 55 or so producers and  and directors the response to the  screening was good  but they said we think you need one  change one big change  and i said what is that and they said  you david smith should be  the narrator and the common thread and  you should tell on the film  something about your background and  where you're going and i said well  that's not my style i'm a behind the  scenes strategic advisor and they said  no you should be  you should tell that story and so  that's kind of how the film got to be  where it is so when i began the film i  had a fundamental question of style  we need to explain things but how do you  do that without being deadly  and it just dawned on me let's connect  humor to animation  to explain a lot of these issues  and also it was a question of what kind  of background visuals  because we're going to have millennial  viewers but we're also going to have  baby boomer  viewers and then people are you know  younger than millennials  so what would they identify with and it  dawned on me that  that we would use humor from 1920 silent  films  that that that humor could be  appreciated by  people of all ages  we're living in an age where we have an  entire generation  growing up that doesn't have a clue  about the role of compromise  in effective governing and so when i  decided who should be in this film i  began looking at people  that i had long admired having been in  washington for 40 years  who were effective at compromise leon  panetta  secretary of defense in the obama  administration was just a master  at compromising at times putting country  before party  james baker did the same on the  republican side  i went back and i said how did we get  voting rights how do we get  civil rights compromise you had  republican liberals from the northeast  meeting with democrats to  get around the southern democratic  resistance to  civil rights and voting rights that's  how it happened this country doesn't  move forward  unless both sides can come together  that's the kind of thing  i said we've got to explore in this film  and so i'm going to go to those people  who have these unusual experiences with  and can explain that to the rest of the  country  picking the cast was a very very  sensitive  process because there are a lot of  people who  privately would say to me what you're  doing is wonderful but i can't do it  because i'm afraid that i'm going to be  attacked because  what you're saying is that both parties  are at fault both parties have been  playing the hate game  trying to gain power and money and they  haven't been putting the country ahead  so  i'd love to participate but i can't on  the other hand i have a lot of people  who came along and said i'm in  people say to me how did you get hawk  newsome to be part of your film he's  the leader of black lives matter greater  new york  and the way i did it i just went up and  saw him in the bronx  i said can we can we have breakfast  together  you know a wall street strategist  you know new filmmaker sits down with  this  guy who comes in he's looked about 6'5  with a motorcycle jacket with  with the black lives matter t-shirt and  we sat down  within five ten minutes as hawk would  say  it was like we had a commonality we  didn't agree with everything but on the  same wavelength  he was telling me about his experiences  with police brutality and he was telling  me about a woman who works for him whose  husband had been  at one of the riots and begged because  he was being suffocated and the idea  it's 100 degrees in  the back of a police car handcuffed you  can't get out and they're not letting  you breathe you're not letting you get  out  and then you know a year later i'm  seeing the coverage of george floyd and  i was struck  it was a trend and i think that hawke  newsome probably utters the most  important  one-liner in the film he says  things have been rough for black  americans  he said america beats the crap out of us  but we still love our country i wanted  people to hear that  because just because you protest i would  peaceful protest  but just because you're a protester  doesn't mean you hate your country it  means  you actually want your country to get  better there has been this  effort to take anyone who's involved in  the social justice  reform effort to paint them as america  haters now there are some who are  american haters and they think the  country is inherently evil  it can never reform or whatever and i  thought it was unfair one  particular network you know tried to  smear hawk as  as kind of a violent monster well if you  meet hawke or if you see him  in the film you say this is a good guy  who's trying to come to terms with  the injustice in a system that he sees  needs to be fixed  well i had read about derrick black who  was the heir apparent to the white  nationalist movement  you know part of the whole kkk white  nationalist  message and now backed off and done a  u-turn from the very early period  in his life he's fed this kind of mental  poison  what made him do the turnaround he said  you know  you change people not by  feeding them arguments you change them  by  changing their community and he said so  here i am secretly the heir apparent to  a white nationalist movement in a  college  where the guy next door is jewish  and my best friend is from south america  and my whole family  religion was they didn't belong in this  country  people asked me how did you gain access  to all these people how did you convince  them to be in the film what did you do  when you were turned down  well i was turned down quite often i  just kept going back  you just have to push and you push and  the more you push  the more people say okay  this is important and so i'll i'll do it  the editorial process is very  interesting because you're dealing with  a lot of data a lot of input  it really gets down to trust can people  who have agreed to be interviewed  can they trust you that you will not  manipulate  their views barry levinson and during  one of his uh  you know times watching the film he said  you know  there are no cheap shots i think that is  why this  film resonates stars and stripes was was  very challenging because in the middle  of filming we had a pandemic  and which kind of changed everything  as i adjusted the film to reflect the  pandemic which was  a challenge but i thought we did it  artfully the idea was  to have a film that would appear  just before and then be part of the  discussion  after the election and the election  would be a rude and bold  statement essentially the country's  divided and if we're not careful  we're going to go into a civil war of  some sort  and that this film is is a stand that  says  we don't need to do that and as someone  who's involved in  for the financial markets from the  highest level i can tell you  that how we are perceived is going to  affect things like the strength of the  dollar it's going to affect the ability  of  and willingness of people overseas to  finance our debt there are a lot of big  issues here that politicians don't think  about  when they spew hate back and forth but  basically can  set the terms for a much weaker economy  decades from now i'm often asked why  doesn't the film  center itself around donald trump and  that's uh  we avoid trump for a particular reason  trump is the master  of the mud pit and he says come into my  mud pit  make it all about me and i said  what if we did a film that doesn't  mention trump  which of course he will hate the most  and think about these issues from the  standpoint of america's long-term future  when i first mentioned that to leon  panetta he said i'm sick of talking  about trump let's talk about bigger  things  mistakes in the economy and mistakes in  the political system  i wanted the film to look at things like  economic humiliation  when i was 14 our house burned down  i was living in baltimore and my parents  didn't have a lot of money  and i lost everything  it dawned on me that change is the one  constant  you cannot assume anything about the  future  and i want the viewer to concentrate on  a visual  that occurs early on the bowel of a ship  a ship  turning over and there's a bell of a  ship it looks like the hindenburg  and people are scrambling on the bottom  of the ship just to survive and my point  was  people never see change until it comes  they never expect it and then it comes  and it's blunt  and it's vicious and it's  life-threatening we assume this country  can take  all kinds of abuse we can do anything we  want and things will stay the same we'll  always be successful  always be have a good economy we'll  always be safe  the one thing that i would hope that  someone from the academy who's watching  this film the one thing they come away  with  is that guy makes a point there's a  sense of urgency right now  that change is inevitable and we may not  like the change  that we're going to face and that's why  we need to  join together and have a serious  discussion about america's future

